WEBVTT

00:00:09.700 --> 00:00:15.690
Now, I'd like to refer to the first graph.
As you can see this is a bar graph measuring

00:00:15.690 --> 00:00:19.850
net sales over the first ten months of the
year.

00:00:19.850 --> 00:00:24.560
You'll notice that sales rose steadily in
the first few months, then there was a marked

00:00:24.560 --> 00:00:31.800
increase in April. They peaked in May at around
3.2 million, and levelled off, then there

00:00:31.800 --> 00:00:37.930
was a dramatic drop in the following month,
followed by a significant increase in August,

00:00:37.930 --> 00:00:41.250
and this trend has continued up until the
present.

00:00:41.250 --> 00:00:43.750
What was the reason for the sudden drop in
July?

00:00:43.750 --> 00:00:49.530
This was mainly due to a drop off in air conditioner
sales - so it's a seasonal effect.

00:00:49.530 --> 00:00:55.060
Could it be a consequence of the negative
effect of the interest rate rise?

00:00:55.060 --> 00:01:03.290
Possibly. Now, if I could draw your attention
to this next diagram. This is a line graph

00:01:03.290 --> 00:01:11.260
of sales - the blue line represents air conditioner
sales, the red line shows heaters. As you'll

00:01:11.260 --> 00:01:18.090
note, air conditioner sales dropped steadily
from January to July, bottoming out then,

00:01:18.090 --> 00:01:25.010
while heater sales experienced a sharp increase
from March to June, then dropped markedly

00:01:25.010 --> 00:01:31.940
from June to July, then declined through to
September, with a pronounced drop in October.

00:01:31.940 --> 00:01:34.470
Does this explain the fluctuation in total
sales?

00:01:34.470 --> 00:01:41.540
Largely. If we look at this pie diagram, you
can see that air conditioners and heaters

00:01:41.540 --> 00:01:48.580
together represent more than half of our total
sales but they vary seasonally, while other

00:01:48.580 --> 00:01:53.080
appliances are fairly steady through the year.
Well, we can't sell air conditioners when

00:01:53.080 --> 00:01:55.970
it's cold. What's the solution?

00:01:55.970 --> 00:01:58.279
Export to Europe and America!

00:01:58.279 --> 00:02:09.740
Easier said than done.
Today we're looking at presenting information

00:02:09.740 --> 00:02:15.310
using charts and graphs. We saw three types
of diagram:

00:02:15.310 --> 00:02:19.580
a bar or column graph
a line graph

00:02:19.580 --> 00:02:25.640
and a pie chart.
Look at how Tan introduced his presentation.

00:02:25.640 --> 00:02:31.640
Now, I'd like to refer to the first graph
- as you can see this is a bar graph measuring

00:02:31.640 --> 00:02:35.069
net sales over the first nine months of the
year.

00:02:35.069 --> 00:02:39.960
Tan says, "I'd like to refer to the first
graph."

00:02:39.960 --> 00:02:46.729
When referring to a diagram or graph, first
direct your audience's attention to that diagram.

00:02:46.729 --> 00:02:50.680
Practise with Tan some phrases to use for
this.

00:02:50.680 --> 00:02:56.629
I'd like to refer to the first graph

00:02:56.629 --> 00:03:01.689
If we have a look at this graph

00:03:01.689 --> 00:03:07.320
If I could direct your attention to the graph

00:03:07.320 --> 00:03:14.860
Looking at the graph on the screen
Let's look at the language Tan uses to describe

00:03:14.860 --> 00:03:19.229
what the graph shows.
You'll notice that sales rose steadily in

00:03:19.229 --> 00:03:26.290
the first few months, then there was a marked
increase in April. They peaked in May at around

00:03:26.290 --> 00:03:32.039
3.2 million, and levelled off, then there
was a dramatic drop in the following month,

00:03:32.039 --> 00:03:37.569
followed by a significant increase n August,
and this trend has continued up until the

00:03:37.569 --> 00:03:39.129
present.
Here's our graph:

00:03:39.129 --> 00:03:46.830
Tan said the sales rose steadily at first,
then there was a marked increase in April.

00:03:46.830 --> 00:03:55.920
This levelled off, then there was a dramatic
drop, and then a significant increase.

00:03:55.920 --> 00:04:03.569
In describing trends, we use two words - one
of those words is a noun or verb.

00:04:03.569 --> 00:04:10.080
For example we may talk about an increase,
or a decrease in numbers. Other words for

00:04:10.080 --> 00:04:17.189
an increase are rise, climb, improvement,
upturn.

00:04:17.189 --> 00:04:24.580
Most of these words can also be used as a
verb: to increase, to rise,

00:04:24.580 --> 00:04:28.790
to climb, to improve.

00:04:28.790 --> 00:04:36.020
Other words for a decrease are fall, decline,
worsening, downturn.

00:04:36.020 --> 00:04:42.380
These also have verbs from them: to decrease,
to fall,

00:04:42.380 --> 00:04:46.119
to decline, to worsen.

00:04:46.119 --> 00:04:52.189
So we say, there was an improvement in the
figures for April, or the figures for April

00:04:52.189 --> 00:04:54.890
have improved.

00:04:54.890 --> 00:05:02.939
There has been a decline in sales since June,
or sales since June have declined.

00:05:02.939 --> 00:05:10.590
But we often add more descriptive words -adjectives
and adverbs. Remember adjectives go before

00:05:10.590 --> 00:05:16.070
nouns, and adverbs go after verbs.

00:05:16.070 --> 00:05:24.070
These describe the change in figures - was
it big or small, fast or slow?

00:05:24.070 --> 00:05:28.959
Other words for a big change are significant,
marked,

00:05:28.959 --> 00:05:34.630
massive, pronounced, substantial.

00:05:34.630 --> 00:05:43.499
Most adjectives can also be made into adverbs,
just by adding -ly or -lee. There is no adverb

00:05:43.499 --> 00:05:48.610
for 'big', but informally we say 'a lot'.

00:05:48.610 --> 00:05:58.760
Other words for small are slight, insignificant,
and their adverbs slightly, insignifantly.

00:05:58.760 --> 00:06:05.559
Other words for a fast or quick change are
sharp, dramatic, sudden

00:06:05.559 --> 00:06:10.559
and again we add -ly for the adverbs.

00:06:10.559 --> 00:06:18.420
And for a slow or medium change, we can use
steady or moderate, and the adverbs steadily

00:06:18.420 --> 00:06:20.790
and moderately.

00:06:20.790 --> 00:06:29.040
Now, try changing the phrases from noun phrases
into verb phrases. For example, if Tan says,

00:06:29.040 --> 00:06:35.939
"There was a dramatic increase in sales" you
say, "Sales increased dramatically."

00:06:35.939 --> 00:06:44.339
Have a try.
There was a steady rise in sales.

00:06:44.339 --> 00:06:47.439
Sales rose steadily.

00:06:47.439 --> 00:06:53.950
There was a significant fall in sales.

00:06:53.950 --> 00:06:56.980
Sales fell significantly.

00:06:56.980 --> 00:07:03.189
There was a slight recovery in sales.

00:07:03.189 --> 00:07:10.070
Sales recovered slightly.
Now let's look at how Tan handles a question

00:07:10.070 --> 00:07:13.469
about the graph.
What was the reason for this sudden drop in

00:07:13.469 --> 00:07:14.489
July?

00:07:14.489 --> 00:07:20.269
This was mainly due to the drop off in air
conditioner sales so it's a seasonal effect.

00:07:20.269 --> 00:07:25.830
Could it be a consequence of the negative
effect of the interest rate rise?

00:07:25.830 --> 00:07:29.899
Here are four useful phrases for describing
causes:

00:07:29.899 --> 00:07:35.140
due to
The drop in sales is due to an interest rate

00:07:35.140 --> 00:07:36.679
rise.

00:07:36.679 --> 00:07:42.210
a consequence of
The drop in sales is a consequence of an interest

00:07:42.210 --> 00:07:44.549
rate rise

00:07:44.549 --> 00:07:49.489
because of
The drop in sales is because of an interest

00:07:49.489 --> 00:07:51.320
rate rise.

00:07:51.320 --> 00:07:56.380
a result of
The drop in sales is a result of an interest

00:07:56.380 --> 00:07:58.709
rate rise.

00:07:58.709 --> 00:08:06.649
How does Tan explain his next diagram?
This is a line graph of sales - the blue line

00:08:06.649 --> 00:08:13.860
represents air conditioner sales, the red
line shows heaters. As you'll note, air conditioner

00:08:13.860 --> 00:08:21.149
sales dropped steadily from January to July,
bottoming out then, while heater sales experienced

00:08:21.149 --> 00:08:28.530
a sharp increase from March to June, then
dropped markedly from June to July, then declined

00:08:28.530 --> 00:08:33.058
through to September, with a pronounced drop
in October.

00:08:33.058 --> 00:08:39.369
He says air conditioner sales 'bottomed out'
in July. This means they reached their lowest

00:08:39.370 --> 00:08:41.159
level.

00:08:41.159 --> 00:08:47.440
Then he says they 'experienced a sharp increase'.
And he says there was a 'pronounced' drop

00:08:47.440 --> 00:08:55.770
in heater sales in October. 'Pronounced' here
means significant, or large. Finally, look

00:08:55.770 --> 00:09:03.270
at how Tan talks about his pie diagram.
If we look at this pie diagram, you can see

00:09:03.270 --> 00:09:08.930
that air conditioners and heater sales together
represent more than half of our total sales

00:09:08.930 --> 00:09:14.880
- but they vary seasonally, while other appliances
are fairly steady through the year.

00:09:14.880 --> 00:09:22.380
Tan says air conditioners and heaters 'represent'
more than half of sales. This means they account

00:09:22.380 --> 00:09:27.210
for more than half of the sales. We could
put this another way:

00:09:27.210 --> 00:09:32.789
More than half of sales are represented by
air conditioners and heaters.

00:09:32.789 --> 00:09:38.070
We could say washing machines represent 15%
of sales.

00:09:38.070 --> 00:09:48.049
Washing machines account for 15% of sales.
Washing machines make up 15% of sales.

00:09:48.049 --> 00:09:52.430
And that's accounted for our time today in
the Business of English. See you next time.